ght literally laugh at in an opinion—it would bring more attention to the silly story and make them look humorless. ### 3. So why does "crazy" = "protected"? Because the legal question isn't “How outraged is the person?” or “How wild is the tale?” It’s “Would a reasonable person believe this is a statement of actual fact?” The more outlandish, the clearer the answer is “No.” A headline like “Elvis Is Alive and Working at a Car Wash in Detroit” is deemed so inherently improbable that it doesn’t even enter the courtroom as a factual claim. A headline like “Elvis’s Grandson Sold Drugs to an Undercover Cop” is different—if false and specific, it’s a factual claim that a reasonable reader might accept. So the craziness is precisely the shield. The tabloids run two business models side‑by‑side: - **High‑risk plausible gossip** (they sometimes get sued and settle). - **Low‑risk absurd fiction** (legally bulletproof, designed to grab eyeballs but not belief). That’s why, despite the stacks of wild headlines, you don’t see a new celebrity lawsuit every week over the really over‑the‑top stuff. The few lawsuits that do happen are virtually always about the quieter, more insidious “true‑sounding” stories. In short: yes, I’m quite sure. The fact that it feels weird is exactly why the law works this way, and why those magazines have such an elaborate, playful visual language—it screams “this is a joke” to anyone with common sense, and that keeps them out of court.
